# vanua

A pipeline-centric workflow GUI core for bioimage analysis.

Named for *vanua* — Fijian for the inseparable unit of land and the people on it —
continuing the Pacific-place naming of Fiji and napari.

## What it is

A shared core for a family of desktop analysis applications. Each application is a
thin **workflow plugin**: an ordered list of steps, their parameters, and the
algorithms behind them. Everything else — theme, application shell, parameter
forms, thread/progress/cancel plumbing, image readers, workspace persistence,
installer tooling — lives here and is written once.

Current consumers:

| App | Domain | Repo |
|---|---|---|
| VasoTrace | 3D lightsheet vessel morphometry | `Organoid-Vessel-Analysis` |
| DendroTrace | 2.5D nerve-fibre morphometry | `hair-follicle-analysis` |
| vanua-viewer | Generic OME-Zarr-focused bioimage viewer | this repo, `vanua.viewer` |

## Gallery: apps an agent built with the vanua skill

Each app below was built end to end by an agent using the vanua agent skill
([`skills/vanua/`](skills/vanua/SKILL.md)) from a single prompt
([tutorials](skills/vanua/examples/README.md)): alignment questions, a
declarative app spec, a recipe of vanua's building blocks, a GUI with a
guided Tutorial / Demo mode, a headless batch runner, and tests. The
screenshots come from the apps' own scripts (`scripts/screenshots.py`).

### HippoVol: hippocampal volumetry (medical imaging, T1 MRI)

*Prompt: plan provided.* Anterior / posterior hippocampal volume per scan,
flagging a scan when the A/P ratio is outside 0.8–1.6.

| Guided demo | Slice + labels | 3D label surfaces | Review: plots + cohort table |
|---|---|---|---|
| ![HippoVol demo mode](examples/skill-apps/hippovol/screenshots/00-demo.png) | ![HippoVol overview](examples/skill-apps/hippovol/screenshots/01-overview.png) | ![HippoVol 3D](examples/skill-apps/hippovol/screenshots/02-3d.png) | ![HippoVol plots](examples/skill-apps/hippovol/screenshots/03-plot.png) |

Data: Medical Segmentation Decathlon, Task04 Hippocampus (CC-BY-SA 4.0);
the screenshots are derived from it and shared under the same licence.

### Nuc3D: 3D nucleus counting (bioimaging, confocal)

*Prompt: one sentence; guided alignment.* Segment, split and count nuclei
in 3D; per-nucleus volume, intensity and centroid; border-touching nuclei
counted but excluded; the stack flagged above 30% border contact.

| Guided demo | Slice + labels | 3D nuclei | Results: summary, histogram, table |
|---|---|---|---|
| ![Nuc3D demo mode](examples/skill-apps/nuc3d/screenshots/00-demo.png) | ![Nuc3D overview](examples/skill-apps/nuc3d/screenshots/01-overview.png) | ![Nuc3D 3D](examples/skill-apps/nuc3d/screenshots/02-3d.png) | ![Nuc3D results](examples/skill-apps/nuc3d/screenshots/03-plot.png) |

Data: scikit-image `cells3d`, provided by the Allen Institute for Cell
Science; Apache-2.0.

## What it is, and what napari is for instead

Two app shapes share this core (docs/decisions.md D12). A **pipeline app**
(CalQTrace, DendroTrace, VasoTrace) is fixed and gated: a versioned workspace,
numbered steps a user cannot skip or hand-edit around, determinate progress,
one recipe. `vanua-viewer` is the third shape, and it is open by design: a
generic OME-Zarr-focused bioimage viewer (Load / Tools / Export, plus a
right-side Rendering dock for per-layer display) where extensions add ops,
readers and a step page.

Neither shape copies napari's plugin architecture, which contributes menu
items and dock widgets with no prerequisite gating and no way to *remove*
capability from the user — a poor fit for a pipeline app, and unnecessary
openness for the viewer, whose extension surface (ops, readers, one step page;
docs/decisions.md D14) is closed enough to freeze into a single installer.
Extension discovery in a shipped build is an explicit manifest, never runtime
pip discovery, which is the specific thing that makes freezing a napari-style
plugin system into a single-file installer awkward.

vanua's installers are real `.dmg`/`.msi` for lab machines with no Python, for
every app built on it, viewer included.

## Architecture

```
vanua.core   Qt-free  -- readers, workspace, typed op registry, recipes,
                         parameter schema, ROI geometry. Runs headless.
vanua.ui     PyQt5    -- theme, shell, generated forms, workers, viewers.
                         Imports core; never the reverse.
```

That boundary is enforced, not aspirational: `tests/test_no_qt_in_core.py` walks
`vanua.core`'s import graph by static analysis and fails on any reference to a GUI
toolkit — including a lazy `import PyQt5` inside a function body — or to
`vanua.ui`.

Three further design commitments, each of which pays for itself immediately and
also happens to be what an agent would need to assemble workflows later:

1. **Recipes are data, not code.** A workflow is a schema-validated document, so
   it can be validated with no display and no Qt.
2. **Ops declare typed inputs and outputs.** The registry is a typed dataflow
   graph, so an invalid pipeline is rejected before it runs rather than twenty
   minutes into a skeletonization. Step gating falls out of the same metadata.
3. **Headless execution is a first-class entry point**, not a test affordance.

See [docs/agent-api.md](docs/agent-api.md) for the contract and its invariants,
and [docs/decisions.md](docs/decisions.md) for the decision log.

## Development

```bash
pixi install
pixi run test              # default suite (slow/gui markers excluded)
pixi run test-core-clean   # the architectural guard on its own
pixi run test-all          # everything, including benchmarks
```

Packaging vanua-viewer into a `.app`/`.exe` + `.dmg`/`.msi` uses a separate
`packaging` environment (PyInstaller is not a default dev dependency):

```bash
pixi run -e packaging generate-spec  # build VanuaViewer.spec from vanua.packaging
pixi run -e packaging build-app      # pyinstaller VanuaViewer.spec
pixi run -e packaging build-dmg      # macOS only; scripts/build_dmg.sh
```

See `docs/decisions.md` D15 for what this pipeline has actually been run and
confirmed against (a real PyInstaller freeze through to a mounted `.dmg`).

The dev environment deliberately carries Qt *and* VTK *and* every reader backend
so the whole core is testable in one place. Consumer apps install narrower extras
— see `[project.optional-dependencies]` in `pyproject.toml`.

## Status

Early. Phase 1 of the extraction: core built test-first, with VasoTrace as the
proving ground and DendroTrace migrating one module at a time. `vanua-viewer`
(`pixi run viewer`) has a layer model, OME-Zarr reader/writer, a network-safe
open policy, a recipe runner and CLI, generic histogram/LUT widgets and
Slice/MIP/Ortho/3D viewers, a themeable shell, an extension system
(in-process and pixi-env runtimes), a GPU compute seam, and a packaging
pipeline verified against a real PyInstaller freeze (docs/decisions.md D15).
Its own shell is Load/Tools/Export plus a right-side Rendering dock showing
the live multichannel composite of every loaded layer -- no numbered
"Layers" step, since per-layer display state is adjusted continuously, not
completed and left behind.
The Tools step does not yet call extension discovery, and no viewer widget
yet asks for a zoom-driven pyramid level (docs/agent-api.md §17/§18).

